Converting an Object to a DTO (Data Transfer Object) without Spring is a common requirement in microservices, legacy systems, or when you want to avoid the overhead of a heavy framework.

Here are the four most effective approaches, ranging from manual to automated.

---

### 1. The Manual Approach (Reflection)
This is the most flexible method. You write a utility class that uses Java Reflection to copy fields from the Source Object to the Target DTO.

**Pros:** No dependencies, total control over mapping logic.
**Cons:** Verbose, slower than modern libraries, requires handling nulls and types manually.

```java
import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;

public class ObjectToDtoConverter {

    public static <T, D> D convert(T source, Class<D> targetClass) {
        D dto = targetClass.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();

        try {
            Field[] sourceFields = source.getClass().getDeclaredFields();
            Field[] targetFields = targetClass.getDeclaredFields();

            for (Field targetField : targetFields) {
                // Find matching field in source
                Field sourceField = findMatchingField(sourceFields, targetField);

                if (sourceField != null) {
                    targetField.setAccessible(true);
                    sourceField.setAccessible(true);

                    Object sourceValue = sourceField.get(source);
                    targetField.set(dto, sourceValue);
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Conversion failed", e);
        }
        return dto;
    }

    private static Field findMatchingField(Field[] sourceFields, Field targetField) {
        for (Field sourceField : sourceFields) {
            if (sourceField.getName().equals(targetField.getName())) {
                return sourceField;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }
}
